
  <sn><md>### Anion Exchange

**Anion exchange in soil** is the `reversible process` by which negatively charged ions (anions) are attracted to and held on positively charged sites on soil particles, and can be replaced by other anions from the soil solution. It is the counterpart to cation exchange, but it is generally `weaker, more variable, and less understood` because most soil particles carry a net negative charge.

**The Basic Reaction**

$$
\text{Soil} - \text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^- + \text{NO}_3^- \rightleftharpoons \text{Soil} - \text{NO}_3^- + \text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^-
$$

An anion held on a positively charged soil site (here $\text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^-$ is exchanged with another anion in solution (here $\text{NO}_3^-$). Like cation exchange, the reaction is reversible and governed by concentration and binding strength.

</md></sn>
