<sn><md>### Why It Happens

Anion exchange requires **positively charged sites** on soil particles. These arise from:

1. **Protonation of surface hydroxyl groups** (–OH) on clay edges and iron/aluminum oxides:
$$
\text{Fe} - \text{OH} + \text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{Fe} - \text{OH}_2^+
$$
This is **pH-dependent** — positive charge increases as pH decreases.

2. **Broken edges of clay minerals** where Al–OH and Fe–OH groups can gain protons.

3. **Iron and aluminum oxides/hydroxides** (goethite, gibbsite, hematite) — these are the main sources of positive charge in many soils, especially highly weathered tropical soils.

4. **Allophane and imogolite** (volcanic ash soils) — variable charge minerals with significant anion exchange capacity.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Key Concepts

**Anion Exchange Capacity (AEC)**
The total quantity of exchangeable anions a soil can hold, usually expressed in cmol(+)/kg or meq/100 g. AEC is generally much **lower than CEC** in most soils because negative charge dominates.

**Positive charge development**
Positive charge on soil surfaces increases when:
- pH is **low** (more H⁺ available for protonation)
- Iron and aluminum oxide content is **high**
- The soil is highly weathered (tropical/subtropical soils)

**Lyotropic series for anions**
Anions differ greatly in how strongly they are held:

$$
\text{OH}^- &gt; \text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^- &gt; \text{SO}_4^{2-} &gt; \text{Cl}^- &gt; \text{NO}_3^-
$$

- **OH⁻** is held most strongly (and is why liming releases adsorbed phosphate).
- **Phosphate** is strongly adsorbed, often by **ligand exchange** (a specific adsorption mechanism), not just simple electrostatic exchange.
- **Nitrate and chloride** are weakly held and easily leached.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Two Types of Anion Retention

| Type | Mechanism | Example | Reversibility |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Exchangeable (non-specific)** | Electrostatic attraction to positive sites | Cl⁻, NO₃⁻ | Readily reversible |
| **Specifically adsorbed** | Ligand exchange, covalent bonding | H₂PO₄⁻, MoO₄²⁻ | Less reversible, stronger |

**Specific adsorption** (also called ligand exchange) is especially important for phosphate:

$$
\text{Fe} - \text{OH} + \text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^- \rightarrow \text{Fe} - \text{H}_2\text{PO}_4 + \text{OH}^-
$$

The phosphate replaces the hydroxyl group and bonds directly to the metal ion. This is why phosphate is much less mobile than nitrate or chloride in soil.

</md></sn><sn><md>### Comparison: Cation vs. Anion Exchange

| Feature | Cation Exchange | Anion Exchange |
|---|---|---|
| Charge of ions | Positive | Negative |
| Soil charge involved | Negative sites | Positive sites |
| Dominant in most soils | Yes | No (except acidic/oxide-rich soils) |
| Capacity | CEC (high) | AEC (low) |
| pH effect | High pH → more CEC | Low pH → more AEC |
| Key ions | Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, K⁺, Na⁺, NH₄⁺ | H₂PO₄⁻, SO₄²⁻, Cl⁻, NO₃⁻ |
| Main minerals | Smectite, vermiculite, humus | Fe/Al oxides, kaolinite, allophane |
</md></sn>